## 2. Linear Blend Skinning (LBS) vs Dual Quaternion Skinning (DQS)

### A. Linear Blend Skinning (Standard GPU Pipeline)
$$\mathbf{v}' = \sum_{i=0}^{N-1} w_i \cdot \mathbf{M}_i \cdot \mathbf{v}, \quad \sum w_i = 1.0, \ N \le 4$$
* **Defect:** "Candy-wrapper" collapse—joints lose volume under extreme twist (e.g. wrist 180° twist, shoulder 90° roll).

### B. Dual Quaternion Skinning (Volume-Preserving)
Represent rigid bone transforms as Unit Dual Quaternions: $\hat{q} = q_r + \epsilon q_d$ where $q_r$ is rotational quaternion and $q_d = 
rac{1}{2} t q_r$.
$$\hat{q}_{	ext{blend}} = 
rac{\sum w_i \hat{q}_i}{\lVert \sum w_i \hat{q}_i 
Vert}$$
* Eliminates candy-wrapper volume loss completely.
* Requires 2x `vec4` attributes per bone transform instead of $4 	imes 4$ matrices in shader uniforms, saving uniform buffer bandwidth.

